{
  "result": "On mornings when the city is still deciding whether to wake, I take my coffee to the window and watch the street assemble below me.\n\nA baker lifts a metal shutter; a cyclist glides past with one hand raised, and a bus sighs at the corner, bright with sleepy passengers.\n\nNothing important seems to happen, yet each small movement gives the day loose stitches, joining separate lives into a temporary neighborhood for a few hours.\n\nI used to believe that meaningful days required grand plans, dramatic changes, or a destination worth naming on a map for curious strangers to follow.\n\nNow I suspect attention is its own kind of journey: not traveling somewhere new, but noticing what was already waiting beside us all along, quietly.\n\nThe coffee cools, traffic thickens, and sunlight reaches the opposite wall, revealing a small plant someone has placed on a windowsill, green against gray brick.\n\nI cannot name the plant, and I do not know who tends it, but its leaves look improbably confident in their little patch of light.\n\nPerhaps this is enough: a cup, a window, a stranger's kindness, a stubborn leaf, and the quiet discovery that the ordinary is not empty yet.\n\nWhen I finally leave, the neighborhood will be louder, and my coffee will be gone, but I will carry that brief morning with me anyway.\n\nMaybe a life is built less from milestones than from these small acts of looking, repeated until the world feels familiar and miraculous at once.",
